00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.710
So we'll start by talking
about SLOs and error budgets.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.810
And if you've seen me talk
about this before, if you've

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.960
seen any of our SREs,
you know anytime

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.670
we give a talk like
this, we always

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.720
start by talking about
SLIs, SLOs, and SLAs.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.050
And it may be review for some
people, but for some people

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:00.920
they get confusing.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.910
It's important to
understand what we mean

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.390
as we refer to each of them.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.889
So we'll spend just a slide
talking about what each of them

00:02:07.889 --> 00:02:08.389
are.

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:11.390
But SLIs are metrics,
SLOs are targets,

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.510
and SLAs are the promises
you make about those.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.070
So we start with SLIs.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.590
The important thing
to know is that this

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.530
is a quantitative measure.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:24.630
There was a time when there
was up and there was down.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.630
There was a time
when is the site up?

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.153
Isitdown.com was
a thing we used.

00:02:28.153 --> 00:02:29.570
And really, that's
how we measured

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:30.820
whether something was working.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:32.300
Was it up or was it down?

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.700
But we've gotten
past that, and now we

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.580
think about defined
quantities that we

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.788
can measure so that we can
all in an organization,

00:02:39.788 --> 00:02:42.080
from the people who wrote
the service to the people who

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.038
were running it, agree
on what the targets are

00:02:45.038 --> 00:02:45.830
we're going to set.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:49.280
So it's an aspect
of the service.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.860
The ones we generally think
of are what we at Google

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.720
call the golden signals.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.420
For anyone who has
read the SRE handbook,

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:00.260
you'll know that there's four
golden signals for a service.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.930
There is traffic,
how much traffic

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.460
is hitting that service.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.663
There is error rate.

00:03:05.663 --> 00:03:07.580
What percentage of those
requests that come in

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.440
are not resulting in errors?

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.283
There's latency, and latency
is not just a single number,

00:03:13.283 --> 00:03:15.200
but rather it's a bunch
of numbers we measure.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.533
It's how long to take a request.

00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:20.270
We measure the time it
takes to serve requests,

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.160
but we do that at
different percentiles.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.560
And the reason is that
the 50th percentile,

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.600
your average request
length, isn't always

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.550
indicative of what's
happening, and what

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:32.210
we tend to see is
that at the tail,

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.225
at the 90th
percentile, the 95th--

00:03:34.225 --> 00:03:35.600
depending on your
traffic levels,

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.290
99th percentile-- you will
often see unacceptable results.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.900
And so we measure
it out there too.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.100
And we often divide use as
SLIs both the 50th percentile

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.590
and the 99th because
you might say, hey,

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:53.000
we want under 200 milliseconds
for the average user.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.020
That might be OK.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.470
But it might not be OK for the
99th to be at 20 milliseconds.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.870
And so it's important to
monitor those and agree

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:02.000
that these are things
we're going to care about.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.550
There's a fourth golden
signal that our SREs refer

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:11.090
to as saturation,
and saturation is

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:12.650
different for
different services.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.510
Sometimes it might
be hardware related

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:16.820
like it might be how much
RAM is being consumed

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.060
or what CPU percentage you're
using, but it can be different.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.500
It might be number of
current active user sessions.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.630
It can be different
for different services,

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.490
and sometimes people
define custom metrics,

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:31.940
and they have their app
actually writing metrics out.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.978
The important part is that
you agree in your teams what

00:04:34.978 --> 00:04:36.020
you are going to measure.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.068
Now Istio, for its part,
gathers most of these.

00:04:39.068 --> 00:04:39.860
It gathers traffic.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:40.818
It gathers error rates.

00:04:40.818 --> 00:04:43.010
It gathers latencies
so that you can then

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:46.790
have discussions about where
you want these metrics to be,

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:51.122
and that's where we get into
service level objectives, SLOs.

00:04:51.122 --> 00:04:54.200
A service level objective,
basically it's a target.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.810
It's what you consider
to be acceptable.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:59.870
It's sometimes a range
you want to stay above,

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.000
like you want your
successful rate

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.850
to be above you say
99.95% of your requests.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.720
It might be a value
you stay below,

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.290
like you want 50%
of your requests

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:17.720
returned in a hundred
milliseconds or less.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.370
And sometimes it ends up
being a range as well.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.880
These things can be defined
very, very granularly

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.250
on each microservice
in your application,

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.400
and it's a good idea to do that.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.860
We believe strongly in
measuring these things

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.860
for every single service
that we deploy, and we do.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.530
We monitor every service
no matter how small.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.400
We monitor these metrics.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:45.680
We are also moving to something
we call user-centric SLOs.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.970
And user-centric SLOs aren't
defined in terms of how long

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:53.240
does it take this particular
service to return a request

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.400
but rather how long
does it take from when

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:59.660
a user clicks, say, a Save
button to when it returns.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.880
And that might
involve lots and lots

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:04.220
of requests on the back end.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.707
It might involve a chain of
requests from the client,

00:06:06.707 --> 00:06:09.290
and it almost certainly involves
measuring it from the client.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:12.800
We're not going to get too much
in user-centric SLOs today.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.425
They're actually
a fairly new thing

00:06:14.425 --> 00:06:15.800
that we're evolving
on in Google.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.008
It's difficult to instrument.

00:06:17.008 --> 00:06:20.150
You might actually have to start
measuring in the client app

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:21.740
itself.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:25.800
But it is an important
concept to know about.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.408
And one thing about an SLO is
you may alert somebody when you

00:06:30.408 --> 00:06:31.700
go out of compliance with them.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.485
You won't necessarily.

00:06:33.485 --> 00:06:35.360
An important concept
that our SREs talk about

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.562
is alert fatigue.

00:06:37.562 --> 00:06:39.770
Just because you've set a
goal and you're not meeting

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.570
it momentarily doesn't mean
you need to wake somebody

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:42.820
up in the middle of the night.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:46.050
We'll talk a little bit
more about alerting later.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.810
And then finally there's SLAs.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:53.570
SLAs are agreements you make
with somebody about your SLO.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.960
When I say you, I mean you're
the producer of it's service.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.650
Sometimes these are informal.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.290
It might be just an
informal agreement

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.290
you have in your
company about, hey,

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.250
we need your service
that we're depending on

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:08.420
to succeed 99.9% of the time.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:11.900
It might be that the worst thing
you get is the ire of that team

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.250
if you violate it.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.160
However, these are
often legal agreements.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.280
And those of you who are Google
Cloud customers have probably

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.920
seen or at least heard about--
we have pages where we document

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.420
exactly how we will intend to
run the service and what we

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:27.880
will do for you if we don't.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:32.730
In that case, it means
generally rebates and money.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:36.200
And these are generally set
on, of course, the SLOs.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.400
So we start with the SLAs.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.290
We set objectives,
and occasionally we

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:42.410
make agreements about
those objectives.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.340
So I said before we used
to talk about uptime a lot,

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:48.023
and uptime was basically how
much time was there total

00:07:48.023 --> 00:07:49.690
and how much that
time was up and what's

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.760
the ratio between
those two, right?

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:55.030
And that made sense when
we were talking about sites

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:57.340
and we were talking
about something

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.200
it was either going
to return or not.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.672
Things are more complicated
now that we deal with services.

00:08:02.672 --> 00:08:04.130
Both of these
graphics, by the way,

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.380
are taken from the SRE handbook.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.880
Now we think more really
about aggregate availability

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.190
where this will take
into account the fact

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:13.310
that a service is global.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.450
It's not a single thing.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:17.110
It's running across the world
in different data centers.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.730
And if it's down in one and
up in another, is that up

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:20.710
or is that down?

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:23.390
Well, aggregate availability
takes that into account.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:26.590
It's the number of requests
that were attempted

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:28.390
in the denominator,
and the numerator

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:30.220
is the total number of requests.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.970
Now interestingly, we
still define our SLAs

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:37.190
in uptime at Google for
many of our services.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.270
And to do that, we combine
them, and we'll say things

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:45.730
like downtime is any five-minute
period where more than 10%

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:48.290
of our requests are failing.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.310
And then we make a
request about downtime--

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.420
I mean we make a guarantee about
downtime and how little of it

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:54.740
we expect there to be.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:58.280
So in reality, we are measuring
aggregate availability,

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:00.530
but we look at that
in discrete chunks

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:03.200
so that we can define uptime and
define downtime and really give

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.383
an SLA that people
can understand.

00:09:05.383 --> 00:09:07.550
Now I want to talk a little
bit about error budgets.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:09.650
So that's SLIs, SLOs, SLAs.

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:11.540
Now let's get into
error budgets.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.030
The point behind budgets
is that every time you

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:17.630
push new software,
you're taking a risk.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:20.692
When your system is up and it's
running and you're in stasis,

00:09:20.692 --> 00:09:23.150
it's probably going to stay
running unless you write memory

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:25.200
leaks, which I know you don't.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.870
But if you don't, then
you're probably OK.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.840
The risky times for software is
when you're doing a deployment.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.050
Every push is risky.

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:37.690
Error budgets exist to
help us manage that risk.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:40.960
And basically you can think of
it as SLO that you can spend.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:42.670
Time that you're within
your SLO does not

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.530
count against your
budget, and time

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:48.850
that you're not within your
SLO counts against your budget.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.910
So you define an SLO over
a long period of time.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:55.450
We typically do this for a
month or often over a quarter,

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:57.820
and we say as long as
you are within budget,

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:59.650
you can continue
to push software.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:02.050
You can continue to take risks.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:04.090
However, that budget
exists over that quarter

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:05.860
to help you manage
that risk and to force

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.360
you to manage that risk.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.420
If every moment you're
out of compliance,

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:14.950
you're spending your budget,
and if you go out of budget,

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:16.340
you can't push new features.

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:18.340
You can still push, but
everything at that point

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.700
can only be pushed to help
improve your infrastructure,

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.980
to help address
the problems that

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:28.510
is making your system go down.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:32.320
So your quality pushes
continue, but your feature

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:33.882
pushes do not continue.

00:10:33.882 --> 00:10:36.340
If you have ever been excited
about a new feature you heard

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:38.660
about, maybe at a conference
like this from Google,

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:40.600
and you were expecting
it in a certain quarter

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:42.520
and it didn't ship
that quarter, it

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:44.470
may be because
engineering is hard,

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:46.668
but it may be that
they ran out of budget

00:10:46.668 --> 00:10:48.460
and they literally
weren't allowed to push.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:50.502
This is something that we
work very religiously--

00:10:50.502 --> 00:10:53.320
I shouldn't say religiously, but
we work very diligently toward.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:57.850
And this is something that
really aligns engineering teams

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.590
and operations teams.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:02.980
The operations team is
really incented to keep

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:05.983
the system up and running
and to build infrastructure

00:11:05.983 --> 00:11:07.400
to keep the system
up and running.

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:10.540
That's what our SRE do.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:12.227
And they'll push
back on the dev team,

00:11:12.227 --> 00:11:14.310
and if the dev team won't
do it, well then they're

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.185
going to push something
bad and they're going

00:11:16.185 --> 00:11:17.380
to run out of error budget.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:18.922
And so here you can
see a chart where

00:11:18.922 --> 00:11:21.390
we've got our SLO is
a dotted line there.

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:22.640
It's a red dotted line.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.800
The time that we're above, we're
not burning through our budget.

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:27.460
And I chose one
where we're burning

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:28.460
through a lot of budget.

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:30.510
And if you look up top there--

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.620
I thought we had
one more build--

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.050
you can see we've
got 125 minutes

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:37.090
remaining in our error budget.

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:38.050
This is April.

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:39.850
There's two months left.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.430
The service is in dire trouble
right now because they've only

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:43.222
got two hours left.

00:11:43.222 --> 00:11:45.070
They've burned through
95% of the budget.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.320
So that's how you use a budget.

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:48.910
It's to manage risk.

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:50.292
And in order to
do it, you really

00:11:50.292 --> 00:11:52.000
have to adopt it for
real, and you really

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.850
have to say no one will
push if we're out of budget

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:56.200
for the rest of the quarter.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:57.783
When you start looking
at it that way,

00:11:57.783 --> 00:12:01.610
it really lets you
manage your risk.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:04.030
On the other hand if it's
towards the end of the quarter

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:05.740
and you've had a
great quarter, you

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:08.530
can afford to push
more frequently.

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.420
One of the original
SREs at Google

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.850
used to say what's the use
to having all these knives

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.593
if we don't take them out
for a spin once in a while?

00:12:15.593 --> 00:12:17.260
When you've got lots
of budget, push it.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:17.920
Push it.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:18.867
Push those features.

00:12:18.867 --> 00:12:20.200
But it lets you manage the risk.

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:22.600
It lets you reason together
as a development team,

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.240
as an operations team.

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:27.790
I talked a little
bit about alerting.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:31.060
Alerts are not necessarily for
when you are out of budget.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.040
Alerts are for when
something's on fire,

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:34.060
when something is down.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:38.010
When your error rate is over 10%
and you're on the spanner team,

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.120
you're getting woken up.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:41.470
That's what alerts are for.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:44.860
Budgets are more like when your
infrastructure needs repair.

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:46.797
If you're running out
of budget for a quarter,

00:12:46.797 --> 00:12:48.880
it tells you you need to
spend some of your cycles

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.890
right now in improving
your infrastructure

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:53.590
and improving your test
processes, improving

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:55.420
your release processes.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:58.280
That's where you should
be spending your time.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:00.740
And a final note
about canaries--

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.690
one of the reasons we
do canaries, of course,

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:05.630
is so that if there's a problem
in a service as we release it,

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:08.600
we don't affect all of
our users at once, right?

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.870
Of course, but these
play into budgets as well

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:14.930
because when I canary my
release and expose it only

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:17.390
to a small number
of users, yes, I

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:20.717
am guaranteeing that
if there's a problem,

00:13:20.717 --> 00:13:22.800
it's only going to affect
a small number of users,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:24.650
but I'm also not
blowing my budget.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.790
If there's a problem and
it affects 100% of my users

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:31.550
for 10 minutes, that might
be my budget for the quarter.

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:35.393
If it affects 1% of my
customers for 10 minutes,

00:13:35.393 --> 00:13:36.560
I might still have a budget.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:38.810
So one of the things
we use canaries for

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:41.300
is to help manage your
budget and to help

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.160
make sure that you
are-- again, it's

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:44.520
really about risk management.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:47.300
It's about making sure that
you catch these problems

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.380
before they affect you overall.

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:54.920
So that's what I have about SLOs
and error budgets in theory.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.670
Now Ruxanda will
talk a little bit

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.130
about what we've
built in the software

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:02.050
to allow you to do this stuff.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:03.910
RUXANDA DANETIU: We
really appreciate

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:07.560
understanding how these SLOs
work and the error budgets as

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:08.060
well.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:10.185
So at this point, we want
to see how these actually

00:14:10.185 --> 00:14:11.690
work in real life.

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:13.780
So what we are actually
trying to do here

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:16.897
is that Stackdriver has worked
really closely with the Istio

00:14:16.897 --> 00:14:19.320
team, and we wanted
to provide a solution,

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:21.400
a service-management
solution, that

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:25.270
can empower users to get out
of the box telemetry that

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:27.010
requires zero setup.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:30.550
Our main goal is to empower
all of our customers

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:34.960
to have one single dashboard
for both management

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:36.310
and observability.

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:39.010
You have the single place
where you can go and look

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.520
at all of your metrics, without
having to worry about agents,

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:44.380
without having to
change context,

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:46.660
without having to think
about this too long.

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:49.010
Everything is out
of the box for you.

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:52.090
So I'm going to welcome
Patrick to the stage.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:55.970
Patrick is the lead engineer
working on service monitoring,

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:58.000
SLO monitoring,
and topology graph,

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.190
and his team has built
a lot of the features

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:01.517
we're going to demo today.

00:15:01.517 --> 00:15:03.100
I had the immense
privilege of working

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:04.910
with him on the past
couple of months

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:06.320
of putting this together.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.500
So while Patrick
sets this up, I want

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.270
to really understand what
we're going to show right now.

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:13.145
It is the alpha product
that we are launching

00:15:13.145 --> 00:15:15.345
at Next for service management.

00:15:15.345 --> 00:15:16.720
So you're coming
here, and you're

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:18.940
going to see a list of
all of your services,

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:23.110
your Istio services that have
deployed to the Istio mesh.

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:27.930
And really what we built
embedded from the beginning

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:29.200
is SLO monitoring.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:32.050
This is one of the things
that we cared about deeply.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:36.700
So at the top you can
easily see which services

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:41.920
have SLO-based alerts firing
or not, which of your services

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:44.860
have SLOs out of your budget.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:46.620
And some services,
exactly as Dan

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:48.120
said, some of these
services, you're

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:49.840
not going to set
up SLOs for them.

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:53.080
You're not going to end up
alerting on them because not

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.260
everything is as
important, and we're

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:57.790
going to talk about
how to prioritize that.

00:15:57.790 --> 00:15:59.710
In addition to this,
you can actually

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.010
review all of your services.

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:04.840
So you get a list of all of
your Istio services right here.

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:07.900
You can easily filter based
on the things you care about,

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:10.450
and you get this out
of the box telemetry.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:12.610
What this view is
missing is that when

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:16.690
you have-- imagine 300,
500 of these services.

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:18.970
It's really hard
to figure out what

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:21.640
to prioritize, what to look
for, how to do root cause

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:23.600
analysis in this format.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:27.820
So what Patrick's team has
built is a apology graph.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.790
This is a way for us
to actually visualize

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:34.000
all of the relationships
between these services.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:38.170
You can actually see at a glance
how all of this information

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.370
gets transferred.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:42.803
If you hover on any
of these services--

00:16:42.803 --> 00:16:44.470
on the left-hand side
you see the legend

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:46.150
that shows you which
one are workloads

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:47.525
and which one are
services so you

00:16:47.525 --> 00:16:49.130
can differentiate between them.

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:50.830
And when you hover
on one of them,

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:53.280
you get actually
highlighted information

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:56.500
of what are the inbound
and outbound services.

00:16:56.500 --> 00:17:00.340
Here you can also see the QPS
based on those relationships

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:01.720
as well.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:04.359
So I spent a lot of time
talking to customers when

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:06.520
we built this
functionality, and we really

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:08.740
wanted to get to kind
of the root cause.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:12.069
How do customers currently
think about their architecture?

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:14.290
And a lot of the
feedback that I received

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:18.819
is that there is a picture, a
really awesome picture that's

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:21.520
been sitting in a folder
for the past six months

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:25.240
of a whiteboard, a PDF
that hasn't been updated

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:26.859
in two to four years.

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:29.500
And every time they onboard
somebody new to the team,

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:32.590
they have to start
taking pen and paper out.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:35.740
The benefit of this, if you
look at the bottom timeline,

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:37.690
it says 4:59 PM.

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:40.660
So what happens here is
that we are extracting

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:43.510
all of this information,
and we give you a view

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:46.090
of your architecture right now.

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:49.570
And this is key because
you might care about how

00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:52.190
your architecture is today.

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:53.680
But let's say it's
Monday morning.

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:54.880
You come into the office.

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.260
You look at a
postmortem, and you

00:17:56.260 --> 00:18:00.040
realize that you had an
incident on Friday at 4:00 PM.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:01.693
And you're reading
the postmortem,

00:18:01.693 --> 00:18:02.860
and you have some questions.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:04.810
You're trying to
evaluate what happened.

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:07.330
So we built this feature--

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:08.470
we call it a time machine.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:09.900
We thought it was a cool name--

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:11.710
where you can go back in time.

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:14.230
You can go and look
at your architecture

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:16.360
at 4:00 PM on
Friday, and you can

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:20.270
see how all of your services
looked around that point.

00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:23.150
And that is great.

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:25.750
However, that just
gives you an idea

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:29.350
of how your world looked
when the incident happened,

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:33.350
but what you care about
is what has changed.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:36.640
So maybe you want to look
at your architecture an hour

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:40.090
before and an hour after.

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:42.480
And you can do that maybe
like switching really, really,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:46.230
really fast and maybe can
get dizzy in the process.

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:48.860
So what the team has
built is this notion

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:52.140
of doing a time
diff between these.

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:53.870
So you're looking
at the cartservice.

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:56.450
This is the service that
you see you have the alert.

00:18:56.450 --> 00:18:59.180
And then you notice that
from the cartservice,

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:02.720
the checkout service right
there, the workload at the top,

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:04.760
is where the information
is coming through.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.120
That's one of the source.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.030
So let's expand
that for a second

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:10.078
and look at the cartservice.

00:19:10.078 --> 00:19:11.120
You look at the workload.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:14.450
You see you have a deployment,
and within that deployment

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:18.150
you notice that you
have these pods.

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:20.690
So OK, this is good information.

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:23.610
You want to see the world, how
it looked before and after.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:27.500
So let's turn the time
diff mode on and be

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:31.370
able to select two
points in time, one hour

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:36.650
before your alert and one
hour after so we can actually

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:43.310
see the differences between
that workload and see

00:19:43.310 --> 00:19:45.200
is there any change there?

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:48.960
Can we actually spot any
difference between those two?

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:51.440
So what we're noticing here
is that an hour before we

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.880
had two pods, and an hour after
the incidents we had five.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:59.420
This is helping you
do root-cause analysis

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:00.920
at a glance--

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:03.560
powerful, visual--
without having

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:06.350
to go through a bunch
of logs, without having

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:07.880
to go through a lot of metrics.

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:11.640
You can see these differences
right at the spot right there.

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:14.160
And you can do this
in different modes.

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:16.770
This is kind of my favorite
one where you can actually

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:21.290
hover over and see as these
changes are being made.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:22.970
And the idea here
is that what we're

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:26.330
trying to empower
our users is to be

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.640
able to do your troubleshooting
as fast as possible

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:32.600
and to do that in the moment if
it's a 3 o'clock in the morning

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:36.340
or to be able to put yourself
in the shoes of your developer

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:42.060
and operator team even if it's
days or months afterwards.

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:44.760
So we've seen that
there is an issue there,

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:49.530
but we know that the alert
happened on the cartservice.

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:51.393
So let's go back to
the cartservice again.

00:20:51.393 --> 00:20:53.060
Let's see if we can
get more information

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:55.500
about the cartservice.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.740
So as we are
exploring this, we're

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:00.710
noticing that the
cartservice itself

00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:02.400
has an alert around that time.

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:04.550
So when you click on it,
we have the capability

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:07.310
to get some minimum
information about it,

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:09.050
and we can also have
this link that says

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:10.410
Go to the Service Dashboard.

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:13.160
So let's explore
where that takes us.

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:16.910
So what we built-- as long as
you're using the service mesh,

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:20.060
every single service
that you've deployed

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.850
will have an automatically
generated out

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:25.280
of the box dashboard for you.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:29.030
Everything that you see right
here requires zero setup.

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:32.270
The only single thing
that you will have to do--

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:35.630
and that is your choice--
is to set up on SLO for it.

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:37.010
So let's look at the top.

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:38.790
We have an event timeline.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.110
This is scoped to one day.

00:21:41.110 --> 00:21:45.380
In red we actually see
every single incident

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:49.040
if it was caused by
an SLO-based alert.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.870
When you hover over
it, you get information

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:54.290
around the alert status, the
time this alert was created,

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:55.730
and the duration.

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:59.090
We have a deep integration
with IRM, the incident response

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:01.610
management tool, that
will link you directly

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:05.000
to that alert for you to be
able to do further understanding

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:06.170
of what happened there.

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:08.990
You can go to your alert
policy as well in order

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:11.540
to make changes to that alert.

00:22:11.540 --> 00:22:14.150
As we're looking to
the tab underneath,

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:16.610
you see that we are
on Overview tab.

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:19.077
These are the two key
things that we prioritized

00:22:19.077 --> 00:22:19.910
when he showed this.

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:23.300
We really cared about
putting SLO health

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:27.500
as the number-one priority
for you to monitor.

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:30.020
And then you also have
at-a-glance metrics

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:32.910
and you have details
around your space.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:35.060
Let's go next to the Health tab.

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:36.820
This is where you're
actually going

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:39.590
to spend a lot of
your time thinking

00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:41.580
about your SLO management.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:47.090
So we've created here three
SLOs, unavailability, latency,

00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:49.280
and a second latency one.

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:52.340
You can see that you get
at-a-glance overview of which

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:55.190
ones are healthy, which
ones have an alert firing,

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:57.510
and which are out
of the SLO budget.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:59.960
So let's open one of them.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:02.240
As an SLO gets
created, you get an out

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:04.490
of the box mini
dashboard for that SLO

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:06.230
that gets generated for you.

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:10.350
You get a service-level
indicator, an error budget

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:12.650
remaining, and compliance.

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:15.170
You get your alert
attached to it

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:18.080
that you can create based
on every single SLO.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:21.440
So if you can collapse this for
a second, what we can actually

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:24.950
see is that our
first SLO doesn't

00:23:24.950 --> 00:23:26.900
have policies attached to it.

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:28.850
You can create SLOs
that you monitor,

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:31.160
and you create SLOs
that you alert on.

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:36.430
The entire creation, editing,
and deleting of an SLO,

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:38.950
our target is that you
need to accomplish that

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:40.610
under 30 seconds.

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:43.030
So let's try to create
an SLO right now.

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:47.950
There are three main features
that you need to add.

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:51.160
You decide your SLO type
and the threshold for it.

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:56.380
You decide on your SLO goal and
then on the compliance period.

00:23:56.380 --> 00:24:00.870
As soon as you finish
this, you can click Submit,

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:01.780
and you can name it.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:04.950
You get to the full name, but
you can make this personal

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:06.690
and name them yourself.

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:08.430
Once this gets created,
what you can see

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:11.440
is that SLO has been
generated immediately.

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:15.900
But more importantly, that
SLO has been evaluated.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:19.260
Because we are able to get
this information directly

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:23.665
from Istio, we can go and look
back and pull this information

00:24:23.665 --> 00:24:27.180
in and do a historical
evaluation of your SLO

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:30.420
and give you a status
within a couple of seconds

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:32.830
after the SLO has been created.

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:36.730
And we can delete
it just as fast.

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:38.680
Let's look at the
Metrics tab next.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:43.360
When we think about metrics,
when we think about monitoring,

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:44.950
there is a lot of
engine deployment

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:46.690
and there's a lot
of back-end work.

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:48.640
Everything that you
see under this tab,

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:50.590
as long as you're
using the service mesh,

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:52.690
all of these metrics
are generated here.

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:55.000
All of the charts
are generated here.

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:57.940
Every time you end up
scoping on the event timeline

00:24:57.940 --> 00:25:04.120
at the top, the time frame,
your charts get updated as well.

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:07.360
If you want to know something
around your metrics,

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:10.400
around an alert, and around
something you care about,

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:12.650
you get all of your
metrics right here.

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:15.250
These are the golden signals
that Dan talked about.

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:19.300
We have request counts, error
rate, latency, request size.

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:22.360
All of this information
comes out of the box.

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:26.010
Let's look at diagnostics next.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:29.640
This is where we have an
integration with logging.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:31.890
At the top of the
widget here you

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:35.520
are actually being able to see
a histogram of all of your logs,

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:39.480
and underneath we displayed
a select number of logs that

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:41.670
have been filtered by severity.

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:45.630
Again, if you're playing and
changing any of the event times

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:48.840
at the top, you're getting
an updated, refreshed view

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:50.910
instantaneously of your logs.

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:52.440
But we know this is not enough.

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:53.850
This is not enough information.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:55.950
It gives you enough
information to know

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:59.450
if you want to decide to look
at the full logging experience.

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:01.500
So let's say we are
intrigued and we

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:05.385
want to look at the
full logging experience.

00:26:05.385 --> 00:26:09.030
This has been generated
immediately for us,

00:26:09.030 --> 00:26:10.950
and we have maintained context.

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:13.290
We are looking at the
exact same service

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.390
as we were looking in
the service dashboard,

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:18.090
and we are using the exact same
time spans from over there.

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:20.310
So we can do advanced
filtering here

00:26:20.310 --> 00:26:22.710
without changing
the context at all.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:24.547
Let's say we spent
some time in logs.

00:26:24.547 --> 00:26:26.130
We found something
interesting, but we

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:28.843
want to go back to the
dashboard and investigate more.

00:26:28.843 --> 00:26:30.510
The next thing we're
going to look about

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:32.650
is the infrastructure.

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:36.030
And this is where we have a very
strong integration with the GKE

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:38.160
team where we are
actually showing

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:41.110
all of the information around
the requests per second.

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:45.060
And also you have access
to be able to directly link

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:49.020
to the GKE pod's details
or to the GKE cluster

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:50.800
as well for more details.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:52.270
So you don't have to change.

00:26:52.270 --> 00:26:54.180
We are going to keep
the context for you.

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.710
So as you're doing all
of your investigation,

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:58.980
you are always
constantly looking

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:00.480
at the things you care about.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:03.300
There is no filtering required.

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:06.930
And lastly, we're going to
look at connect services.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:11.130
When we talk about root-cause
analysis, one of the key things

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:14.310
that we always ask
is is it my problem

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:16.320
or am I down the
funnel from something

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:18.690
bigger that I need
to pay attention to?

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:22.350
And we are able to show you only
the services that are inbound

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:24.570
and the services
that are outbound

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:27.090
to the particular
service you care about.

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:29.910
And let's say that it's one
service that you care about,

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:32.820
so maybe the front-end
service we want to explore.

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:36.510
You can actually go from
here and start this process

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:37.720
all over again.

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:39.420
So now we open the
service dashboard

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:41.610
for the front-end
service, and it gives you

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:43.680
the same visibility
and information

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:46.740
as we had before for
checkout service.

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:49.530
So hopefully this is not going
to be a rabbit hole of looking

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:51.780
at every single
service, but it's really

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:55.850
going to help you narrow down
the things you care about.

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:59.430
So thank you so much,
Patrick, for giving the demo,

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:00.900
and thank you so
much for your team

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:03.640
that ended up building
all of this amazing work.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:05.700
So I want to welcome next Pod.

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:10.884
Can you give us
access to the slides?

00:28:10.884 --> 00:28:12.680
OK, perfect.

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:18.920
So Pod is a
service-reliability engineer

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:24.110
working at Schlumberger, and
we are in a privileged position

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:26.570
to have been working
very closely with Pod

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:29.420
for a couple of quarters now.

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:38.570
And Pod has had early
access to our alpha program.

00:28:38.570 --> 00:28:41.180
And we want to talk
to you a little bit.

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:43.460
We want to do this a
little bit more informal,

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:46.700
and we want to learn a little
bit about how Schlumberger's

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:48.950
architecture looks like.

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:51.110
PARDEEP SANDHU:
Thank you, Ruxanda.

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:52.940
Well, in Schlumberger
we used to have

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:58.440
a custom solution that allowed
us to both deploy and monitor.

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:01.010
However, this was very
difficult in the lift and shift

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:02.480
to the cloud.

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:05.060
We initially focused on
infrastructure monitoring

00:29:05.060 --> 00:29:06.380
with Stackdriver.

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:09.650
However, this proved very
difficult for our teams.

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:14.120
We have both dev and
ops working together.

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:16.670
Our main goal at Schlumberger
is to bring the communities

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:18.590
together and create
an environment where

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:21.230
both our dev and
operations teams

00:29:21.230 --> 00:29:23.643
can collaborate and
work closely together.

00:29:23.643 --> 00:29:25.310
RUXANDA DANETIU: So
what's the direction

00:29:25.310 --> 00:29:27.830
where Schlumberger's next?

00:29:27.830 --> 00:29:29.320
PARDEEP SANDHU: We
wanted to target

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:33.140
a platform for modern-day
development that was flexible

00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:37.200
and can empower us for the
hybrid cloud environment.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:39.590
So we turned to Google
to take advantage

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:43.460
of the service-management
capabilities built

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:47.600
into the platform rather
than build those ourselves.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:49.580
RUXANDA DANETIU: And
so I want to understand

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:54.500
what was the main reason why
you decided to use service mesh?

00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:57.530
PARDEEP SANDHU: Well, there were
three main reasons we wanted.

00:29:57.530 --> 00:30:01.530
One was to have a
Google-native managed solution.

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:06.860
The other was telemetry, and the
other was new-features rollout.

00:30:06.860 --> 00:30:08.690
On the SRE team at
Schlumberger, we

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:12.080
care deeply to smartly
invest our time

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:15.170
and do effective work
for the organization.

00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:18.110
As a result, we did not
want to focus our efforts

00:30:18.110 --> 00:30:23.180
on managing our own Prometheus,
Grafana, or Istio stacks.

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:26.390
We needed to have a managed
solution because this

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:29.640
will empower us to focus
on what's important

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:32.910
and is a differentiator
across our team.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:34.670
Secondly, we wanted
a solution that

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:36.770
offered powerful telemetry.

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:38.660
We wanted monitoring
capabilities

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:42.050
that could help us reduce the
workload for our developers

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:46.370
because the telemetry from Istio
goes straight to Stackdriver

00:30:46.370 --> 00:30:48.410
out of the box, so
our developers don't

00:30:48.410 --> 00:30:51.200
need to write that much code.

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:53.210
For us, what was
really important was

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:55.070
to have the golden
signals work just

00:30:55.070 --> 00:30:59.210
like that without any
extra developer resources

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:04.440
and be able to create SLOs
without any homebrew solutions.

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:05.400
It just works.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:07.980
Without any
configuration or setup,

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:12.690
we have charts, logs, and
SLOs all in one dashboard.

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:15.150
We have a single
pane of glass that

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:19.470
can help us monitor and
diagnose problems further.

00:31:19.470 --> 00:31:21.900
Thirdly, it's great
when multiple teams

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:24.870
can work on the same
platform, especially when they

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:26.860
come from different angles.

00:31:26.860 --> 00:31:30.060
For example, our architects
and product owners

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:31.590
were looking into
how to roll out

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:35.110
new features for a
subset of customers.

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:39.720
Istio has traffic management
so we can canary new features.

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:42.630
These capabilities were
key in our decision

00:31:42.630 --> 00:31:46.020
to adopt Istio and GKE.

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:49.660
In the end, there's no
perfect solution out there.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:52.590
However, the combination
of Istio and the alpha

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:57.270
dashboards, we ended up with
about 80% of what we wanted,

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:01.620
and we thought that was
pretty good if not great.

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:04.230
RUXANDA DANETIU: And so Pod,
you mentioned that SLOs ended up

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:06.240
becoming kind of a
key differentiator

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:09.750
for you making the decision
to adopt the service mesh.

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:12.300
So I wanted to know,
how do you decide?

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:14.430
What is the magic
number of SLOs?

00:32:14.430 --> 00:32:17.285
How many SLOs do you build
for your organization?

00:32:17.285 --> 00:32:19.410
PARDEEP SANDHU: Well, that's
a really hard question

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:23.400
to answer because we do not
come up with a target in mind.

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:27.000
It's not like we say we want
10 SLOs and everything should

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:28.770
fit into that number.

00:32:28.770 --> 00:32:32.520
We actually spend a lot of time
across our functional teams

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:35.400
and try to organically come
up with a number that can help

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.130
our teams do their best work.

00:32:38.130 --> 00:32:41.430
For example, we bucket
services together

00:32:41.430 --> 00:32:44.490
so we can focus on a
small number of services.

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:48.290
We do not create SLOs for
each and every microservice.

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:50.550
However, we do take
an holistic approach

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:53.565
to the practice of SLO creation.

00:32:53.565 --> 00:32:55.940
RUXANDA DANETIU: So tell us
more a little bit about that.

00:32:55.940 --> 00:32:59.180
How do you decide if
a particular service

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:01.933
is worthy of having an SLO?

00:33:01.933 --> 00:33:03.350
PARDEEP SANDHU: I
don't think it's

00:33:03.350 --> 00:33:07.160
about a service being
worthy or not having an SLO.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:09.140
We care about all our services.

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:13.380
However, we make decisions
based on our customers needs.

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:16.250
We have a user-journey
approach to SLOs.

00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:18.710
For example, one
of our key actions

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:22.200
is for the user to be able
to log in and use our app,

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:24.260
so the availability of
our front-end service

00:33:24.260 --> 00:33:26.460
is absolutely essential here.

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:29.000
However, the front-end
service talks to a number

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.960
of back-end services.

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:33.560
So here we create an
SLO for the availability

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:38.600
of the front end and the SLOs
for the back end it depends on.

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:42.350
So we can easily troubleshoot
in case of an incident.

00:33:42.350 --> 00:33:44.360
Before we used to
create probes to see

00:33:44.360 --> 00:33:48.440
if our back-end service was
down via alert notifications.

00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:52.380
Now we can just create SLOs
for those back-end services.

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:54.760
RUXANDA DANETIU: So you
just mentioned user journeys

00:33:54.760 --> 00:33:59.340
and being able to go into
your app and see the data.

00:33:59.340 --> 00:34:02.810
So how do you decide on a
relevance of a user journey?

00:34:02.810 --> 00:34:04.810
How do you prioritize
which user journeys

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:06.398
are going to have SLOs?

00:34:06.398 --> 00:34:07.940
PARDEEP SANDHU:
Traditionally we used

00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:11.060
to look at logs to determine
how the users were interacting

00:34:11.060 --> 00:34:14.210
with our systems and any
problems that they were having.

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:18.290
However, we have now adopted a
much more comprehensive effort

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:22.199
around user journeys and
focusing on the customer.

00:34:22.199 --> 00:34:24.290
This is a team
effort first of all.

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:27.139
Today we meet with our product
owners and subject-matter

00:34:27.139 --> 00:34:29.719
experts, and in all
these conversations

00:34:29.719 --> 00:34:33.139
we discuss what's critical
from a user's point of view.

00:34:33.139 --> 00:34:35.780
The whole process is
extremely thoughtful

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:38.389
because this is the
foundation of everything

00:34:38.389 --> 00:34:42.980
we will build afterwards in
terms of monitoring and SLOs.

00:34:42.980 --> 00:34:45.080
Based on all this
information we collect from

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:48.320
our cross-functional teams, we
can prioritize the things we

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:49.830
should focus on--

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:53.780
for example, when should I wake
up in the middle of the night?

00:34:53.780 --> 00:34:57.170
So in any service we
can degrade many things.

00:34:57.170 --> 00:35:00.800
However, there are some things
that cannot and should not be

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:06.590
degraded because the entire user
experience is reliant on them.

00:35:06.590 --> 00:35:08.810
So we always ask
ourselves, what is

00:35:08.810 --> 00:35:11.690
fundamental part of the system?

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:13.470
What is crucial?

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:16.230
What is absolutely necessary?

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:20.660
So for each user journey, we
create several SLIs and SLOs

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:23.070
and monitor all
those SLOs together

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:26.755
so we can see the entire
user journey in one place.

00:35:26.755 --> 00:35:28.130
RUXANDA DANETIU:
So you mentioned

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:29.360
you use a journey approach.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:31.730
Do you create any
other types of SLOs

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:34.827
that are not customer
facing, for example?

00:35:34.827 --> 00:35:35.660
PARDEEP SANDHU: Yes.

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:38.630
We have a two-prong
approach to SLOs.

00:35:38.630 --> 00:35:41.630
First and foremost are
the customer user journey

00:35:41.630 --> 00:35:45.590
driven SLOs because
we should know

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:48.395
before the customer does
if we have a problem

00:35:48.395 --> 00:35:50.090
and we should fix it.

00:35:50.090 --> 00:35:56.150
Secondly are the back-end SLOs
or the system-journey SLOs

00:35:56.150 --> 00:35:59.740
because we need better
contracts between our teams.

00:35:59.740 --> 00:36:02.990
At Schlumberger, we care about
external customers as much

00:36:02.990 --> 00:36:04.980
as our internal customers.

00:36:04.980 --> 00:36:08.070
So we have contracts
between the organizations,

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:11.630
and we apply the same level of
care to our internal contracts

00:36:11.630 --> 00:36:14.240
as we do to our external users.

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:17.160
We call this the
friends-and-family contract

00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:18.650
because if you
break your contract,

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:23.060
you're going to hear about
it again, again, and again.

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:25.910
So if a back-end service
has a clear dependency

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:29.430
for an internal
or external user,

00:36:29.430 --> 00:36:32.060
this is something we
care deeply about.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:34.560
It has to be up and running.

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:37.050
Our culture at
Schlumberger is to empower

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:40.920
many of the dev teams to be
as autonomous as possible.

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:44.520
And if a team wants autonomy
in our organization,

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:46.920
then that team needs to
maintain the contract

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:49.950
with the rest of the
teams and keep their SLOs

00:36:49.950 --> 00:36:52.037
as part of that deal.

00:36:52.037 --> 00:36:54.120
RUXANDA DANETIU: So you
mentioned the introduction

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:57.160
of internal contracts and
having SLOs based on them.

00:36:57.160 --> 00:37:00.492
How has that changed the
culture at Schlumberger?

00:37:00.492 --> 00:37:01.950
PARDEEP SANDHU: We
definitely think

00:37:01.950 --> 00:37:04.450
it has changed for the better.

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:07.000
For example, in the
past we would have--

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:08.490
how shall I describe--

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:12.060
emotional discussions
when things go wrong,

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:15.360
and most of these conversations
would happen too late.

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:17.340
So now the main
difference is that we

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:20.280
can talk across teams
with actual data

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:22.530
to back those conversations.

00:37:22.530 --> 00:37:26.250
This way we are now working
as a data-driven team,

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:30.300
and things are a lot more
clear and a lot less personal.

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:35.170
Having access to good data can
help us make better decisions,

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:40.570
communicate our intentions
and needs better as well.

00:37:40.570 --> 00:37:42.780
RUXANDA DANETIU: So
I wanted to know--

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.240
Dan talked a lot
about error budgets,

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:48.630
and is that something
that Schlumberger uses?

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:50.100
PARDEEP SANDHU: Yes we do.

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:52.830
They don't look great right
now in our dev environments,

00:37:52.830 --> 00:37:55.720
but we don't expect them to.

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:57.360
But what's important
is that now we

00:37:57.360 --> 00:38:01.310
have the capabilities to see
them and reason about them.

00:38:01.310 --> 00:38:03.850
Before, the idea of
SLO or error budgets

00:38:03.850 --> 00:38:07.350
were just theory that we
read in many different books,

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:10.560
but we had no good
way of showing those.

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:12.400
Now everyone can see them.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:15.300
If an internal or an external
dependency we depend on

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:18.750
goes down and a feature
in our app starts failing,

00:38:18.750 --> 00:38:22.620
you can visually see how you're
blowing through your budget.

00:38:22.620 --> 00:38:25.800
With these insights, we can
talk to the product owners

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:28.200
and agree upon a deal
when engineering should

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:31.828
focus on reliability tasks.

00:38:31.828 --> 00:38:33.370
RUXANDA DANETIU: So
I wanted to know,

00:38:33.370 --> 00:38:36.430
you've been using the
alpha for quite a bit now,

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:39.790
and we wanted to know what
is the next features that you

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:42.350
would need to be
build in the platform?

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:44.350
PARDEEP SANDHU: We're
looking forward to the day

00:38:44.350 --> 00:38:48.160
when there's no manual setup
in any of the SLOs or error

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:49.470
budget.

00:38:49.470 --> 00:38:51.970
I would love to see this
all automated in a way

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:53.420
to create them.

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:55.720
We want all this
as config as code,

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:59.680
even the configuration of the
creation of things like this.

00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:02.830
So dashboards, SLO
setup, everything

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:05.410
should be something we
can generate automatically

00:39:05.410 --> 00:39:07.098
and check into code.

00:39:07.098 --> 00:39:09.640
RUXANDA DANETIU: So what are
the next steps for Schlumberger?

00:39:09.640 --> 00:39:10.900
You've been using this alpha.

00:39:10.900 --> 00:39:12.070
You've been testing it.

00:39:12.070 --> 00:39:14.590
What do you intend to do next?

00:39:14.590 --> 00:39:17.290
PARDEEP SANDHU: Well, I
work on a team Schlumberger

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:21.550
where we use Transformer names
for our software projects.

00:39:21.550 --> 00:39:25.690
I'm lucky enough to work on
the Bumblebee project, the most

00:39:25.690 --> 00:39:29.710
loved transformer, but
I get the opportunity

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:32.710
to work on many new
technologies and try

00:39:32.710 --> 00:39:36.850
to work out how these will
best benefit our organization.

00:39:36.850 --> 00:39:40.040
We call ourselves the cool kids
of Schlumberger due to the fact

00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:43.980
that we get to play with the
new technologies out there,

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:46.050
but we've reached a
point with our engagement

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:49.950
with Istio where I need to
show to many other teams

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:52.140
and evangelize this
across Schlumberger a lot

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:54.400
more broadly.

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:56.970
We need to make an internal
culture shift in order

00:39:56.970 --> 00:40:01.560
for us to adopt the SLO
framework wholeheartedly

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:03.840
and broadly at Schlumberger.

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:06.500
Our main barrier right
now is that this is alpha,

00:40:06.500 --> 00:40:09.672
and we want to adapt this into
production as soon as possible.

00:40:09.672 --> 00:40:11.130
RUXANDA DANETIU:
Thank you so much.

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:13.148
So Dan and I have
some work to do.

00:40:13.148 --> 00:40:14.940
I really appreciate
your time, and I really

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:16.470
appreciate
Schlumberger committing

00:40:16.470 --> 00:40:20.380
of using this as an
early alpha tester.

00:40:20.380 --> 00:40:22.590
So thank you so
much for your time.

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:26.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

